{"id":"managers-per-100-staff","title":"Managers per 100 other employees","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"How many management-class employees does each department have for every 100 other employees, where did that ratio rise most, and what share of the pay bill goes to management classes?","finding":"Citywide, people paid in management classes rose from 1,091 in fiscal 2013 to 1,727 in fiscal 2026: from 3.1 to 4.6 for every 100 other employees, and from 4.9% to 6.5% of all pay ($365.3 million in fiscal 2026). The share of staff in management classes rose most in Human Rights Commission (2.9% to 17.7%), Health Service System (6.1% to 18.9%) and Retirement System (18.7% to 30.4%).","how":"City payroll file, fiscal 2013 to 2026. Management classes are the 97 classes the file itself places in the job family “Management” (the Manager, Deputy Director and Department Head grades and named chiefs and directors), plus the transportation agency’s own Manager classes, which the file lists under transit. A person counts once per department and year if paid in such a class at all; everyone else paid by the department is the comparison. Departments with fewer than 50 people are left out; a department created after 2013 is compared from its first year with 20 people. Board seats, elected offices and the Superior Court are left out. Ranked by the rise in the management share of staff.","limits":"Headcounts include part-time, seasonal and temporary staff, so a department that cut seasonal jobs shows a higher ratio without adding a manager; the pay-share columns are steadier. A rise can also follow work being contracted out, which removes front-line staff but not the people who manage the contracts. Department totals for spending against filled positions are in the screen “Spending up, filled positions down” under waivers and exceptions, and are not repeated here.","columns":[{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"since","t":"Compared from fiscal year","type":"year"},{"